The recursive helper processes one position at a time and returns three facts for that part of the array: the length of its starting run of ones, its ending run, and its best run anywhere inside. When two parts are joined, a run crossing the middle is also checked.

For `[1, 1, 0, 1]`, the left part contributes a suffix of `2`, while the right part has a prefix of `1`. The zero prevents those values from joining, so the final answer is `2`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    findMaxConsecutiveOnes(nums) {
        const solve = (left, right) => {
            if (left === right) {
                const value = nums[left] === 1 ? 1 : 0;
                return {
                    length: 1,
                    prefix: value,
                    suffix: value,
                    best: value
                };
            }

            const middle = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
            const first = solve(left, middle);
            const second = solve(middle + 1, right);

            const prefix = first.prefix === first.length
                ? first.length + second.prefix
                : first.prefix;
            const suffix = second.suffix === second.length
                ? second.length + first.suffix
                : second.suffix;
            const crossing = first.suffix + second.prefix;

            return {
                length: first.length + second.length,
                prefix: prefix,
                suffix: suffix,
                best: Math.max(first.best, second.best, crossing)
            };
        };

        if (nums.length === 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        return solve(0, nums.length - 1).best;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each array position is combined through the recursion tree once.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursive call stack.

The recursive split is useful for seeing the array as smaller pieces. Each piece keeps its length, the run of ones at its start, the run at its end, and the best run inside it. When two pieces meet, their ending and starting runs may form one larger run.

This also covers an array of one element. The code does not modify `nums`.

```go
func findMaxConsecutiveOnes(nums []int) int {
    type segment struct {
        length int
        prefix int
        suffix int
        best   int
    }

    var solve func(int, int) segment
    solve = func(left, right int) segment {
        if left == right {
            value := 0
            if nums[left] == 1 {
                value = 1
            }
            return segment{
                length: 1,
                prefix: value,
                suffix: value,
                best:   value,
            }
        }

        middle := (left + right) / 2
        first := solve(left, middle)
        second := solve(middle+1, right)

        prefix := first.prefix
        if first.prefix == first.length {
            prefix = first.length + second.prefix
        }

        suffix := second.suffix
        if second.suffix == second.length {
            suffix = second.length + first.suffix
        }

        crossing := first.suffix + second.prefix
        best := first.best
        if second.best > best {
            best = second.best
        }
        if crossing > best {
            best = crossing
        }

        return segment{
            length: first.length + second.length,
            prefix: prefix,
            suffix: suffix,
            best:   best,
        }
    }

    if len(nums) == 0 {
        return 0
    }

    return solve(0, len(nums)-1).best
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursion stack.

A small running count is enough here. The current count grows when the value is `1` and goes back to zero at a `0`. The maximum is updated after every element, so a streak at the end is handled without a special second pass.

For `[1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1]`, the values of `current` are `1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 3`, and the answer becomes `3`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findMaxConsecutiveOnes(int[] nums) {
        int current = 0;
        int longest = 0;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (value == 1) {
                current++;
                longest = Math.max(longest, current);
            } else {
                current = 0;
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the array is scanned once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version finds each block of ones as a separate range. First, the left index skips any zeros. Then the right index moves across the block of ones, and its length is compared with the best length found so far.

The input vector is only read, not changed. For an array such as `[0, 1, 1, 0, 1]`, the blocks have lengths `2` and `1`, so the result is `2`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findMaxConsecutiveOnes(vector<int>& nums) {
        int longest = 0;
        int index = 0;

        while (index < static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            while (index < static_cast<int>(nums.size()) && nums[index] == 0) {
                index++;
            }

            int start = index;
            while (index < static_cast<int>(nums.size()) && nums[index] == 1) {
                index++;
            }

            longest = max(longest, index - start);
        }

        return longest;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each position is passed by the indexes at most once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

